WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:01.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I'm
here with Bob from SeMI.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.147
And today, we're talking
all about knowledge graphs.

00:00:04.147 --> 00:00:07.026
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:09.420
Bob, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.170
BOB VAN LUIJT:
Thanks for having me.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.887
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
First things first.

00:00:11.887 --> 00:00:12.970
What is a knowledge graph?

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.120
BOB VAN LUIJT: So
a knowledge graph

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.565
tries to store real-world
entities in a data object.

00:00:20.565 --> 00:00:22.440
And what we tried to do
with knowledge graphs

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.320
is to actually try to understand
how the real world works

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.995
and derive knowledge rather
than just data from the way

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:30.120
that we stored information.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:30.760
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.820
Now, that's different from
a traditional database

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.380
which just stores information.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:35.210
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yes.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.880
So, for example, let's say that
you would store information

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:39.970
about a movie.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.300
Then there's all these
relations that you

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.790
might have in your head
about the movie, maybe actors

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.260
or locations or things
that come to mind.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.980
But if you store that in
a traditional database,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.220
a lot of context just gets lost.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.495
It's just a database--

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:56.370
You could argue that a
database is just dumb.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.132
[INAUDIBLE] It's a dumb
way of storing information.

00:00:59.132 --> 00:01:00.840
What you try to do
with a knowledge graph

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.210
is to actually keep
that information

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.930
and keep that context so that
you get a 360-degree overview

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.910
of what you're actually
storing and that people

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:14.070
can query for the knowledge
rather than the raw data

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:14.798
information.

00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:17.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it sounds
a lot like a traditional graph

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:17.880
database.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.543
But you're also adding in this
knowledge or learning aspect.

00:01:20.543 --> 00:01:22.210
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yes,
that's a good point.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.750
So the graph part
is one could argue

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.000
that everything
that we store has

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.890
a relation to something else.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.320
So, for example,
like, the movies.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.530
If you would look at a movie,
a movie has certain actors.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.340
And they're born
somewhere, and-- well,

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.290
et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.020
So that is there's
always a relation.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.960
So that's the graph part.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.480
What the knowledge part
adds on top of that is like,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.520
can we actually try
to derive knowledge

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.570
from that information?

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:54.840
So let's say that you would have
an actor who is born in Paris.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.560
But you now query for which
actors come from France.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.060
[INAUDIBLE] actually
says, oh, wait,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.435
now I need to show
this actor who

00:02:00.435 --> 00:02:02.050
was born in Paris
because there's

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.717
a relation between
Paris and France.

00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:05.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
Weaviate is the product

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.590
that you've built as
a knowledge graph.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.289
What makes Weaviate special
among knowledge graphs?

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.600
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yeah, so
I would argue that there

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.900
are three things there.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.850
So the first thing is is that
we don't store data objects

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.940
in a traditional
way, so in a table

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.710
or in a traditional graph.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.460
We store it in a space.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.750
And this is a semantic space.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.720
So every time we add
a new data object,

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.000
we try to determine what
does that object mean?

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.090
What does it represent
in the real world?

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.810
And we place it in a space.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.850
So, for example, with
the actor example,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.460
if you would have an actor
who was born in Paris,

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.880
then it would be closely
related to France.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.260
So that's how we
store it as one.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.050
The second thing that
we do is that we're

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.700
completely API-based.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.000
And that is also where
we stop, if you will.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.440
So we try to enable others to
build these semantic systems.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.690
And thirdly, that is
something that we learned

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.290
to the semantic aspect is
that we can now also do

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.530
automatic classification,
which basically means that,

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.310
to stay with the
example of France,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.920
that if you would have a
country with the name France

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.900
and a city with the name
Paris, the knowledge

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.945
graph can actually automatically
derive-- hey, wait a second.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:18.390
That Paris is the
capital of France.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right, so that
learning aspect comes back in.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.950
How does it actually
do the learning?

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.590
I mean, you're inserting the
data by using data objects.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.840
But then it's got to figure
out how these are related.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.132
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yeah, that's
a great question, actually.

00:03:30.132 --> 00:03:31.650
So the interesting
thing is like,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.460
how do we define learning?

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.950
So we have the data
science aspect of learning.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.505
But we also have the
engineering aspect of learning.

00:03:38.505 --> 00:03:40.380
And one of the problems
that we want to solve

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.810
is that every time when
you add a new data object,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.060
we didn't want to retrain
the machinery learning model

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.700
because it now takes
about 48 hours to train.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.370
So just imagine if you want
to add 1,000 data objects.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.410
Well, that's undoable.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.220
So what we learned was that
the machinery model that we use

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.660
has all these vector positions.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.750
So those are representations
of concept in that space.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.020
And what we do is that the
moment that you add data,

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.350
we try to algorithmically
determine where

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.190
to place it in this space.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:13.050
So our learning, if you will,
is not necessarily retraining.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.670
It's just trying
to understand where

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.730
do we need to place that object
in the space, which makes it

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.459
very fast from an engineering
perspective, which

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.019
results in a better UX.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.410
So a user doesn't
really notice that they

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.880
are working with a
data solution that

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.090
incorporates both the machine
learning model and a storage

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:33.590
mechanism.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.715
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, so
how does it actually do?

00:04:35.715 --> 00:04:36.848
I mean, you have the data.

00:04:36.848 --> 00:04:37.890
You're able to add it in.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.330
It figures out the new things.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.483
But without retraining,
it seems very hard

00:04:41.483 --> 00:04:43.650
that it would be able to
learn these new properties.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.690
BOB VAN LUIJT:
Yeah, so there are

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.260
three ways how we can do that.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.940
So we have the
out-of-the-box knowledge,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.732
which we like to call
the common knowledge.

00:04:50.732 --> 00:04:53.070
So we train it on
open data sets.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.750
So in English,
for example, think

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.200
about Wikipedia, the Wiktionary,
but also scientific articles,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:58.750
et cetera.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.670
So that's what we like to
call the common knowledge.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:02.760
Now you can do two things.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.260
So there might be
a situation where

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.930
you have a very specific domain
knowledge that you want to add.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.010
So think about the use cases
in banking, oil and gas

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.020
industry, health
care, et cetera.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.510
So what you can do
to the RESTful API,

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.190
you can literally add
a natural language.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.890
So you can say I'm going
to now teach you a concept.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.660
These are the words
of the concept.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.660
And this is the description
in common knowledge,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.300
because if you think how
you and I learn new words,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.650
that's how we do it.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:34.150
To children, we explain in
common language new concepts.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.670
So what happens
under the hood is

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.280
that we take that concept
and a description.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.070
We calculate a new position
in the vector space.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.257
And that's where that
new concept lives.

00:05:43.257 --> 00:05:45.090
So if you now start to
add that objects that

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.840
talk about that concept,
that's how we give it

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.110
its semantic understanding.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.777
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
you're able to kind

00:05:50.777 --> 00:05:52.290
of teach it, the new concepts.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.390
And then it makes
those new associations

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.993
without having to do this new
layer of retraining, basically.

00:05:56.993 --> 00:05:58.410
BOB VAN LUIJT:
Exactly, because we

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:03.120
wanted to have this UX
aspect is number one for us.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.810
And then speed plays a role.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:06.547
So how fast can we do this?

00:06:06.547 --> 00:06:08.130
And that's the
beautiful thing, again,

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:11.637
of the factorization
of concepts and words

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:13.970
because we can calculate with
it, which means it's fast.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:15.930
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what's the
case where a knowledge graph

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.130
might not be a good solution?

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:18.672
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yeah,
so, for example,

00:06:18.672 --> 00:06:20.340
if you have a lot
of streaming data

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.500
or if you have a lot of
logs, for example, that you

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.940
need to search too fast--

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.910
what we see in practice
is that often you

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.820
have a traditional database
and a knowledge graph.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.020
And they live together and then
you solve problems together.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.430
So or, for example,
transactions at a bank.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:39.410
Then you want to store them
in a transactional fashion.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.160
But if you now want
to search through them

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.780
or find specific
things, then that's

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.750
where the knowledge graph comes
in and takes that information

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.125
and stores it in
a different way.

00:06:47.125 --> 00:06:48.125
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:06:48.125 --> 00:06:50.340
So this isn't supposed to
replace databases out there

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.270
as a whole.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.430
But it's a new layer of
looking at your data.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.722
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yes, that's,
indeed, what I would argue.

00:06:55.722 --> 00:06:59.848
It's just a completely different
way of looking at data.

00:06:59.848 --> 00:07:01.390
However, I would
like to say, though,

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.640
if people would like to
change our data infrastructure

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.160
with Weaviate, I'm all for it.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.336
[LAUGHS]

00:07:06.336 --> 00:07:07.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
it's really exciting

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:08.135
to hear about the space.

00:07:08.135 --> 00:07:09.600
So thank you very much
for coming in, Bob.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:10.642
BOB VAN LUIJT: Thank you.

00:07:10.642 --> 00:07:12.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

